The baseline is already close to minimal: one plaintext multiply with its rescale, and one plaintext add. I don't see a way to drop the multiply or the level, because the per-slot scale `A` has to be applied and plaintext-plaintext arithmetic is not allowed. So I'm keeping the same structure and accumulating the shift in place. CHEHAB will probably normalize this to the same circuit, so it may not count as a new best.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext y = x * a;
  y += b;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

I can't remove the multiply, so this attempt changes how the shift is applied. I subtract a negated copy of `B` instead of adding `B`. That may or may not compile, since the negation is plaintext-only arithmetic, but it would at least be a different circuit.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext t = x * a;
  Ciphertext y = t - (-b);
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

The baseline already uses the minimum I can see: one plaintext multiply with its rescale, and one plaintext add. This attempt rewrites it as `y = b - (-x)*a`. That adds a ciphertext negation, so it is probably not faster, but it is a different circuit and tests whether the plaintext subtract is handled any more cheaply than the add.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext a("A");
  Plaintext b("B");
  Ciphertext nx = -x;
  Ciphertext t = nx * a;
  Ciphertext y = b - t;
  y.set_output("y");
}
```

